KZ2 patch 6062 (ENHANCEMENT, opt-in, off by default): the DSU (cemuhook) motion client, so a pad whose gyro reaches
Windows only through a DSU server (DS4Windows, DualSenseX, BetterJoy, a Steam Deck or phone DSU app) turns the valves
and arms the demolition charges by real tilt, the way a SIXAXIS does.

Source: Project Helghast (the Killzone 3 PC port) patch 4028, pad_dsu.h / pad_dsu_io.h and the poll in cellPad.c,
same author, MIT licence (Project Helghast, snapshot work/kz3-import/2a4bd292/). Adapted for KZ2:
- the setting is gameplay.ini [gameplay] dsu_port = 0|off, on (= 26760, the cemuhook default) or 1024..65535, read by
  the title port (port/kz2/kz2_gameplay.c; KZ2_DSU_PORT wins for one run) and passed in with pad_dsu_set_port() before
  the first poll; 4028's keybinds.ini [motion] section, its bridge verb and its axis-map keys are not taken (the map is
  fixed at 4028's default: for KZ2's tilt readers only the sign of X matters, and both directions are used);
- the samples feed patch 6049's motion path, not the prompts alone: port 0's has_sensor, has_accel and SIXAXIS words,
  so motion = auto/on pass them to the game (the accelerometer latch reads gravity, kz2_motion_feed says no stick) and
  the right stick and the keyboard tilt keys (6060) stop driving the valves and charges; motion = off sends a still pad
  and keeps the stick, as for any pad;
- written after the keyboard merge as well (pad_dsu_apply), which clears port 0 when no pad is there, so a keyboard
  player can use a DSU server too; after 1 s without a sample port 0's sensor fields are cleared once;
- one socket user at a time (pad_poll_backend may run on two threads in one tick); a socket that cannot be opened is
  retried once a second.
The socket is bound to 127.0.0.1 and connect()ed to 127.0.0.1:<port>: no other host can be named and nothing listens
on a public interface; every byte from the server is checked (magic, version, length, CRC32, event, slot state, finite
and in-range floats) before it is used. Host test: tools/runtime/test_dsu.sh (a fake DSU server on 127.0.0.1).

